Date: Sat, 16 Jan 93 18:08:13 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re:  META: Carol's Two Questions

Carol raises an interesting question about the nature of consciousness.
We all know that we are conscious, and that we are made of matter.  It
follows that matter can be conscious.  Does it make sense then to say,
as Carol quoted Dyson, that even the smallest bit of matter has some
tiny amount of consciousness?

It's pretty tough to imagine either ruling this out or confirming it!
But I think the issue may become important when we can make intelligent
software.  If it is conscious, we should be more hesitant to be cruel to
it.

But in asking the question, I think the most important factor will not be
whether it is made of matter, or how many electrons or other particles go
into it.  This should not be that relevant - if we could build computers
out of photons that worked the same way as matter-based ones, they could
be conscious, too, IMO.

The real issue is pattern and function.  Consciousness seems to depend on
a complex neural network in our brain.  Just any random arrangement of
the neural "wiring" would _not_ lead to a conscious brain (just put an IMO
("in my opinion") after every sentence, OK?).  Only a specific
arrangement, one which has evolved over billions of years, leads to what
we know of as consciousness.  Electrons do not share that structure; they
are simple little bundles of electrical charge.  So on these grounds I'd
say they are not conscious, not even a little bit.

Consciousness is also a property of the brain as a whole, and not of its
individual parts.  Each neuron is not conscious, it's the brain which is
conscious.  This suggests a problem with the reductionist approach of
saying that electrons must be conscious since the conscious brain contains
electrons.

When we try to decide whether the AI programs of the future are
conscious, we will not be able to use the simplistic reasoning that Carol
quotes Dyson as giving, not when we have serious ethical questions to
decide.  We will have to get into the nitty-gritty details of exactly
what kinds of functional systems can be conscious, rather than ascribing
a mystical universal consciousness to all matter.

Hal Finney

