Date: Sun, 17 Jan 93 07:59:03 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

>Even a perfect legal system couldn't make a pure-restitution
>policy effective as a deterrent -- particularly if the thief
>had a good investment opportunity.

>   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

This analysis fails to take note of the caught criminal's loss of
reputational capital.  

Even if the victim is only made no worse off then before, the process of
restitution is not a zero-sum transaction from the criminal's point of
view.  The caught criminal will, thanks to systems for measuring and
conveying legal trustworthiness in a PPL, suffer the loss of countless
opportunities to associate with third parties.  In many circumstances
(hiring, housing, offering credit, admitting into a private community,
covering with insurance, etc.) people will choose not to deal with those
who have criminal records, or demand higher rates to compensate for the
risks of doing so.  

Caught criminals thus end up suffering a great deal more than just the
costs of compensating their victims.  But note that the costs associated
with loss of reputational capital qualify as neither restitution nor
punishment.  Rather, this spontaneously ordered response to guilt is a
species of ostracism.  Though often overlooked by advocates of PPL, I must
emphasize that the effects of ostracism would be powerful and real. 
Ostracism may be the worst of all the calamities to befall a caught
criminal.



