that tails are the most popular hairstyle.  They are worn by Max More,
David Krieger, Tim Starr, Anton Sherwood, Rob Michels, and myself (among
others).  I'm sure many more of you sport tails, too; it seems to be the
hairstyle of choice among computer folk.

Extropian principles:  Growing a tail is Extropian because it expresses the
principle of boundless expansion (of hair).  It also supports a far greater
range of self-transformational hair styles than do more conventional cuts;
you can wear your tail up in a bun, loose like a lion's mane, tight up top,
loose down low, etc.  A tail also provides the alert Extropian with an
opportunity to sport the latest nuggets of hair managment technology
(berets, pins, etc.).

Considerations of utility:  I finally decided to grow a tail when I decided
that it was the cheapest and easiest way to style my hair.  Wearing your
hair short requires frequent trips to the barber.  And short haircuts are
not necessarily easy to manage.  I found that keeping my flattop neat
required gel, for example.  But with long hair, I visit the barber seldom
or never and do no more than tuck it under a hat or tie it back each
morning.  Soon, I will have enough hair to achieve the ultimate in hair
management technology:  a system of two clamps, one fixed at the end of my
tail and the other, releasable clamp, set at varying points on the tail's
length.  I'll spare you the details, but I assure you that it will achieve
new heights of efficiency and aesthetics.

Conspiracy theory:  Long hair was the preferred style of the radical
libertarian rebels who founded the U.S.  Jefferson, Adams, Madison,
Franklin, . . . they all wore tails.  But this fact is conveniently being
forgotten by the statist quo.  Consider, for example, the revolutionaries'
portraits on paper currency.  Their heads are carefully turned to hide
their wild locks  (but what about portraits on coins?).  And I scarely need
to mention that arch-statist organizations like the military, Secret
Service, and FBI all demand short hair.  Is this merely for the sake of
neatness?  NO!  Long hair, drawn back tight and tied close, is even
smoother and cleaner than a crewcut.  The *real* reason statists hate long
hair?  It serves as a counterweight on the back of the head, and thus tends
to tilt one's gaze upward.  Statists prefer downcast eyes and bowed heads. 
Libertarians prefer upward gazes and, thus, free long hair. 

I've been wanting to post a message about Extropian hair for some time now,
but have held the topic in reserve to boost list frivolity levels.  Things
have been getting a little hairy out there (in there?) lately, so it seemed
time to address matters hirsute.  So, before you brush-heads and bald folks
start crying for my scalp, keep in mind that I am just trying to do my part
to lower hackles.  You shouldn't feel left out, since one of the first
benefits of genetic therapy will be the abolition of the baldness gene. 
That alone is reason to relish the future!



