From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Immortalist conservatism

Some people have claimed that immortalists should be very conservative
about taking life-threatening risks, since an immortalist who risks death
risks giving up an eternity of life.  The weaknesses of this argument have
been well documented by actual immortalists who post to this list.  I want
to address another sort of conservatism, however:  conservatism with regard
to long-term commitments.  I argue that immortalists should have a
considerably greater immunity to this sort of conservatism than deathists
have.

When a deathist considers a long-term commitment, she must take account of
the fact that her choice may well constrain the rest of her life.  When she
chooses a career, for example, she must keep in mind that she may not get a
chance to try alternative careers before she dies.  She will probably get
only one opportunity to master a particular field of work, and will thus
choose careers very conservatively.

An immortalist, on the other hand, can afford to take greater risks with
regard to long term commitments.  He realizes that he can spend 30 years in
engineering, rising to the top of his profession, and then go on to start
anew as a painter if he pleases.  He will thus tend to be less conservative
in choosing a career.

Arguably, conservatism with regard to long term commitments has a much
greater impact on one's life style than conservatism about life-threatening
behavior.  Thrill seekers don't usually put their lives on the line every
day; rather, they go to school and work like everyone else.  What you
study, how you earn a living, where you live . . . all these long term
commitments shape your life in powerful ways.  Deathists sacrifice their
freedom to choose risky but attractive long term commitments. 
Immortalists, on the other hand, can cheerfully enter into such
commitments, secure in the knowledge that they'll always get another
chance.

A closing note:  this argument does not necessarily apply with regard to
long term commitments to enter into relationships with other immortalists. 
Your immortalist friends and lovers will be with you a *long* time!  Does
this imply that an immortalist will be unduly conservative about such
choices?  I don't think so, but I leave that argument for another day.



